# System Design Blog Posts

Reference implementation: `content/system-design/urlshortner.md`

## Repo Layout

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `content/system-design/_index.md` | Section landing page |
| `content/system-design/<topic>.md` | One post per design (e.g. `rate-limiter.md`, `news-feed.md`) |

URL pattern: `/system-design/<filename>/`

## Hugo Front Matter

```yaml
---
title: "Designing a <System> at Scale"
date: 2026-06-26T10:00:00+00:00
draft: false
description: "One-line summary for SEO and social previews."
tags: ["system-design", "<topic>", "distributed-systems"]
categories: ["System Design"]
---
```

Set `draft: true` while writing; `draft: false` when ready to publish.

## Required Sections (11)

Use numbered `##` headings matching the URL shortener post:

1. **Requirements and Goals** — MVP functional table, premium/optional features, NFRs (scale, availability, latency, read/write ratio)
2. **Back-of-the-Envelope Calculations** — Daily traffic, avg/peak RPS, storage, bandwidth (show math in tables)
3. **API Design** — Overview table (always visible) + collapsible endpoint blocks via shortcodes (see below)
4. **Data Model** — Tables/entities + Mermaid `erDiagram`
5. **High-Level Architecture** — Mermaid `flowchart LR` (entry → services → storage → async pipeline)
6. **Core Algorithm / ID Generation** — Uniqueness, ordering, or partitioning strategy
7. **Database & Scaling** — Tech comparison table, choice, scaling stages (`flowchart TD`)
8. **Caching Strategy** — What to cache, strategy (cache-aside / write-through), sizing
9. **Capacity Planning** — Component table (pods, memory, brokers, network)
10. **Design Decisions + Failure Modes** — Summary table + what breaks and how to mitigate

**Intro** (before section 1): 1–2 paragraphs — what the system does + dominant trait (read-heavy, write-heavy, real-time, etc.).

Separate major sections with `---` horizontal rules.

## API Design (Collapsible Endpoints)

Keep §3 scannable: **overview table always visible**, request/response/errors collapsed per endpoint.

Shortcodes live in `layouts/shortcodes/` (`api-endpoint`, `api-request`, `api-response`, `api-errors`, `api-notes`).

Reference: `content/system-design/urlshortner.md` §3.

### Structure

1. **Overview table** (markdown, not collapsed) — all endpoints at a glance
2. **`api-endpoint`** per route — method badge + path + one-line description always visible
3. **`api-request`** — JSON body, headers, field table (omit if no body, e.g. simple GET)
4. **`api-response`** — success payload; params: `code`, optional `label`
5. **`api-errors`** — status code table (see **Shortcode delimiters** below)
6. **`api-notes`** — optional visible footnote (design rationale, not collapsed)

### Shortcode delimiters (`<` vs `%`) — required

Hugo wraps **standalone block shortcodes** (`{{< ... >}}`) in a bogus placeholder `<h2 id="hahahugoshortcode...">`, which breaks section collapsibles (fake § boundary between API and Data Model).

| Location | Syntax | Example |
|----------|--------|---------|
| **Inside** `api-endpoint` | `{{< api-errors >}}` … `{{< /api-errors >}}` | Per-endpoint error table |
| **Standalone** after all endpoints (shared error codes) | `{{% api-errors %}}` … `{{% /api-errors %}}` | Site-wide HTTP errors under `**Common HTTP error codes**` |

**Correct — nested (urlshortner pattern):**

```markdown
{{< api-endpoint method="POST" path="/v1/links" desc="Create short URL" open="true" >}}
...
{{< api-errors >}}
| Code | Condition |
| `400 Bad Request` | Invalid `long_url` |
{{< /api-errors >}}
{{< /api-endpoint >}}
```

**Correct — standalone shared errors (after closing last `api-endpoint`):**

```markdown
**Common HTTP error codes**

{{% api-errors %}}
| Code | Condition |
| `400 Bad Request` | Validation failure |
| `429 Too Many Requests` | Rate limit exceeded |
{{% /api-errors %}}
```

**Wrong — standalone with `<` delimiters:**

```markdown
**Common HTTP error codes**

{{< api-errors >}}   <!-- DO NOT: Hugo injects bogus h2 -->
...
{{< /api-errors >}}
```

Never put a standalone `api-errors` block between `## 3. API Design` and `## 4. Data Model` using `{{< >}}` syntax.

### Template

```markdown
## 3. API Design

| # | Method | Path | Purpose |
| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | POST | `/v1/resource` | Create resource |

{{< api-endpoint method="POST" path="/v1/resource" desc="Create resource" open="true" >}}
{{< api-request >}}
```json
{ "field": "value" }
```
| Field | Required | Notes |
| `field` | Yes | Description |
{{< /api-request >}}
{{< api-response code="201" label="Created" >}}
```json
{ "id": "abc" }
```
{{< /api-response >}}
{{< api-errors >}}
| Code | Condition |
| `400 Bad Request` | Invalid input |
{{< /api-errors >}}
{{< /api-endpoint >}}
```

- Set `open="true"` on the first endpoint only — expands its Request panel by default
- Use representative JSON; truncate repetitive fields with `...`
- Group related routes under `###` headings when a post has many endpoints

## Architecture Diagrams (Zoom / Lightbox)

Mermaid diagrams render inside a **click-to-expand** frame with a lightbox viewer:

- **Click** any diagram (or press Enter when focused) to open fullscreen
- **Scroll** to zoom, **drag** to pan, **Esc** to close
- Toolbar: `+` / `−` / Reset (re-fit) / Fit
- Title auto-picked from the nearest preceding `##` / `###` heading

No author changes needed — all fenced ` ```mermaid ` blocks inside `.sd-collapsible-content` get this automatically.

### Inline preview vs lightbox (layout behavior)

Wide diagrams (§5 `flowchart LR`, §4 `erDiagram`) behave differently in the two views:

| View | Behavior | CSS / JS |
|------|----------|----------|
| **Inline** (`.diagram-frame`) | Natural SVG width preserved; horizontal scroll if wider than content column | `diagram-lightbox-head.html`: `.diagram-frame .mermaid { overflow-x: auto }`, `svg { max-width: none }` |
| **Lightbox** (modal) | Clone scaled to **fill the viewport** on open | `extend_footer.html`: `prepareSvgClone()` + `fitToStage()` |

**Do not** re-add `max-width: 100%` on inline `.diagram-frame .mermaid svg` — it squashes wide HLD diagrams into unreadable thumbnails.

### Lightbox SVG sizing — critical (do not regress)

**Symptom if broken:** Clicking "Click to expand" shows a tiny diagram centered in a large modal (especially §5 HLD and §4 ERD).

**Root cause:** Mermaid compresses the inline SVG (`width="600"` etc.) to fit the page column, but the SVG `viewBox` stays large (e.g. 5000×800). Naïve `cloneNode(true)` + `fitToStage()` using `viewBox` dimensions applies a small `scale()` to the compressed pixel width → diagram appears ~100px wide in the modal.

**Required implementation in `extend_footer.html`:**

1. **`prepareSvgClone(svg)`** — on modal open:
   - `cloneNode(true)`
   - Remove `width`, `height`, and `style` attributes from the clone
   - Set `width` / `height` from `viewBox.baseVal` (via `getSvgDimensions()`)
2. **`fitToStage()`** — compute `scale = min(availW / svgW, availH / svgH)` from viewBox dimensions
   - No upper cap on scale (removed `1.5` limit) — diagrams must scale **up** to fill the modal
   - Double `requestAnimationFrame` before fit so modal layout is measured
3. **Reset button** — calls `fitToStage()`, not `scale = 1`

**Author rules for diagrams:**
- Use standard Hugo/Chroma fences: ` ```mermaid ` (not indented inside lists unless intentional)
- Do not wrap Mermaid in HTML or shortcodes
- Diagrams inside collapsed §4 / §6 sections still render (lazy init on section open)
- Wide `flowchart LR` HLD diagrams are expected — lightbox handles readability; inline uses horizontal scroll

## Section Collapsibles (Auto)

On every system design post, `##` sections and `###` subsections are wrapped in expand/collapse panels (same chevron UX as API toggles):

- **§1–§11** — collapsed by default; each header shows an **Expand** pill (HLD excluded)
- **§5 High-Level Architecture** — **always fully visible** (no section toggle; diagram lightbox still works)
- Intro paragraphs before §1 stay visible

Implemented in `layouts/partials/section-collapsible.html` — no markdown edits required for toggles themselves.

## Page Enhancements (Layout — do not break)

Client-side features run in this order from `layouts/partials/extend_footer.html`:

1. `initSectionCollapsibles()` — wrap `##` / `###` in `<details>`
2. `renderMermaidBlocks()` — convert `pre > code.language-mermaid` → `.diagram-frame` + Mermaid SVG
3. `initDiagramLightbox()` — click-to-zoom on `.diagram-frame`

**Critical constraints when editing layout JS:**

| Rule | Why |
|------|-----|
| **Mermaid must run even if collapsibles fail** | Separate `try/catch` per step in `renderPageEnhancements()` — never let one failure skip the other |
| **DOM order: `insertBefore` before `appendChild` when moving headings** | In `section-collapsible.html`, anchor `<details>` with `insertBefore(section, h2)` *while h2 is still in the DOM*, then move h2/h3 into `<summary>`. Reversing this throws `NotFoundError` and breaks the entire page |
| **Per-section try/catch in `wrapSections`** | One bad section must not abort wrapping for §2–§11 |
| **Mermaid selector** | `.sd-collapsible-content pre > code.language-mermaid, ... code[data-lang="mermaid"]`; skip blocks already inside `.diagram-frame` |
| **`unwrapShortcodeHeadings()`** | Strips leftover `h2[id^="hahahugoshortcode"]` — prefer fixing markdown (`{{% api-errors %}}`) at source |
| **Lazy Mermaid on toggle** | `.sd-section` / `.sd-subsection` `toggle` listener re-runs `mermaid.run` for unprocessed nodes when opened |
| **Lightbox: `prepareSvgClone()`** | Strip clone `width`/`height`/`style`; set dimensions from `viewBox` — never clone raw SVG into modal |
| **Lightbox: `fitToStage()`** | Scale from viewBox vs stage rect; no max-scale cap; double rAF before measure |
| **Inline diagram CSS** | `overflow-x: auto` + `svg { max-width: none }` in `diagram-lightbox-head.html` — do not squash wide HLD |

**Layout file map:**

| File | Role |
|------|------|
| `layouts/partials/section-collapsible.html` | Section/subsection `<details>` wrapping |
| `layouts/partials/section-collapsible-head.html` | Collapsible CSS |
| `layouts/partials/extend_footer.html` | Mermaid init, diagram lightbox, enhancement orchestration |
| `layouts/partials/diagram-lightbox-head.html` | Lightbox CSS |
| `layouts/system-design/single.html` | Wraps post body in `.sd-collapsible-content` |
| `layouts/shortcodes/api-*.html` | Collapsible API panels |

## Mermaid Rules (v11)

| Do | Avoid |
|----|-------|
| Quote edge labels: `\|"GET /v1/resource"\|` | `\n` in unquoted labels |
| Curly braces in labels: `{code}` | Reserved/special chars in node IDs |
| Use `<br/>` for line breaks in nodes | Ambiguous IDs like `Redis`, `Kafka` as node names |
| camelCase subgraph IDs: `subgraph readPath["Read Path"]` | Unquoted multi-line labels |
| Cylinder nodes: `[("PostgreSQL<br/>Primary")]` | Static images when Mermaid suffices |

**Required diagram types per post:**
- `erDiagram` — data model
- `flowchart LR` — architecture
- `flowchart TD` — scaling stages or request flow

## Workflow

1. Sketch in draw.io (requirements → API → schema → architecture → capacity)
2. Create `content/system-design/<name>.md` using the 11-section template
3. Convert diagrams to Mermaid (no static image unless Mermaid cannot express it)
4. Preview: `hugo server -D` — hard refresh (`Ctrl+Shift+R`) and verify checklist below
5. Set `draft: false` when ready to publish

### Preview checklist (every post)

| Check | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| §1–§11 headers | Bordered cards with **Expand** pill (§5 HLD always visible, no toggle) |
| §4 Data Model | `erDiagram` renders as SVG inside `.diagram-frame`; **Click to expand** fills modal (not tiny centered thumbnail) |
| §5 High-Level Architecture | `flowchart` renders as SVG; inline scrolls horizontally if wide; expand fills viewport |
| API §3 | Overview table visible; endpoint panels collapse/expand |
| Standalone error codes | No bogus `h2` between §3 and §4 — use `{{% api-errors %}}` |
| Diagram lightbox | Reset/Fit re-centers diagram; scroll zooms; no console errors on expand |
| Browser console | No `NotFoundError`, no uncaught DOM exceptions |

Spot-check reference posts: `urlshortner.md`, `distributed-rate-limiter.md`.

## Writing Style

- Use markdown tables for requirements, calculations, comparisons, and capacity
- State concrete numbers (RPS, storage GB, pod counts) — never vague "lots of traffic"
- Separate read and write paths when the system is asymmetric
- Offload analytics/async work off the hot path (e.g. Kafka → analytics store)
- Name services by responsibility (e.g. Write Service vs Read Service), not generic "API"
